<h2 id="h-what-the-species-xt-is-built-for">Who the Bear Species XT RTH Is Built For</h2>
<p>The Species XT is Bear&rsquo;s value-focused hunting bow. It is not trying to top the speed charts. Instead it targets three types of buyers:</p>
<ul class="checks">
  <li><strong>New bowhunters</strong> who want a complete package and a forgiving frame while they learn good form.</li>
  <li><strong>Shorter-draw adults and older teens</strong> who need a draw length below 25", which rules out many adult hunting bows.</li>
  <li><strong>Experienced hunters on a budget</strong> who want a dependable treestand or blind bow, or a backup rig for camp.</li>
</ul>
<p>The 30-inch axle-to-axle length is compact enough for ground blinds and tight treestand setups, and the 6.75-inch brace height is longer than most speed-focused bows. That combination rewards hunters who shoot inside about 40 yards, which covers most whitetail hunting.</p>

<h2 id="h-draw-cycle-and-performance">Species XT Performance: Speed, Draw Cycle and Let-Off</h2>
<p>At 320 fps IBO, the Species XT is a moderate-speed bow. Remember that IBO figures use a 30-inch draw, 70-pound draw weight and a light 350-grain arrow. If you shoot a 27-inch draw at 55 pounds with a 425-grain hunting arrow, your real speed will be well below 320 fps. That is true of every compound bow, and it is not a reason to worry about deer-hunting performance.</p>
<h3 id="h-single-cam-system">The Single-Cam System</h3>
<p>Retailers describe the Species XT&rsquo;s single cam as smooth drawing, and single-cam designs are generally simple to live with: there is no cam-to-cam timing to keep in sync, which makes the bow easier to maintain for owners who do not live at the pro shop. The trade-off is that single cams usually give up a little top-end speed compared with aggressive dual or hybrid cams.</p>
<h3 id="h-let-off-and-holding-weight">80% Let-Off and Holding Weight</h3>
<p>With 80% let-off, a bow set at 60 lb drops to about 12 lb of holding weight at full draw. That is light enough to wait out a deer stepping into your lane, but firm enough that you will not feel the string creeping forward. Many coaches like this level for newer shooters because it encourages a solid back-wall feel.</p>
<p>Before you pick a draw weight, think about energy, not just speed. Run your planned setup through our calculator to see what you will actually deliver downrange.</p>
